<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gdynia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gdynia is a port city on the Baltic coast of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>. A fishing village when Poland regained access to the sea in 1920, it was developed by the Second Polish Republic from the 1920s into a modern port independent of the Free City of Danzig. By the late 1930s it was one of the busiest ports on the Baltic, and with Gdańsk and Sopot it now forms the Tricity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gdynia.jpg" alt="Gdynia" width="320" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kontrola · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:00_sea_towers_(April_2018).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Gdynia is historically and culturally part of Kashubia and Eastern Pomerania. It lies in a conurbation with the spa town of Sopot and the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>, which together form the Tricity, a metropolitan area of around a million inhabitants. In prehistoric times the area was the centre of the Oksywie culture. In the late 10th century the region was united with the emerging state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a>, and the district of Oksywie was first mentioned in 1209, as Oxhöft. For centuries Gdynia remained a small fishing village, and by the 20th century it attracted visitors as a seaside resort.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Polish government decided to build a major seaport at Gdynia in the winter of 1920, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-soviet-war">Polish–Soviet War</a>. German dockworkers in the Free City of Danzig had struck and refused to unload military supplies sent from the West to aid the Polish army, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> concluded that it needed a port it controlled economically and politically. A special committee began building the city in 1925, city rights were granted in 1926 and tax privileges for investors in 1927. Growth became rapid after 1928, with a new railway station and post office, and by 1939 the population had grown to over 120,000.</p><p>Gdynia was the site of fierce Polish defence during the German invasion of September 1939. The Germans captured the whole city on 14 September and occupied it until 1945, carrying out mass arrests of some 7,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> on 15 and 16 September. The occupiers set up prisons and camps, and Poles were deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a> or executed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> captured the city on 28 March 1945 and it was restored to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Settlers arrived from destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Wilno</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lwów</a>, and Greek refugees from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-civil-war">Greek Civil War</a> also came through the port. Protests in and around Gdynia in 1970 contributed to the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a> movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Gdynia is a seaport on the Baltic coast of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and the largest city in Poland that is not a voivodeship capital. Its modernist centre, laid out in 1926 by Adam Kuncewicz and Roman Feliński as a grand maritime façade for the newly independent state, is built around an axis that runs from 10 Lutego Street through Kościuszko Square to the Southern Pier. Its architecture shows monumentalism, early functionalism and interwar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modernism">modernism</a>. The oldest building is the 13th-century church of St Michael the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkhangelsk">Archangel</a> in Oksywie, and two museum ships, the destroyer ORP Błyskawica and the sailing ship Dar Pomorza, are moored in the harbour. The port is the home of the 3rd Ship Flotilla of the Polish Navy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gdynia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Great Depression Eugeniusz Kwiatkowski's plans built the port of <b>Gdynia</b>, the Polish Coal Trunk-Line from Upper Silesia, and the Central Industrial Region, with the new steel town of Stalowa Wola.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-polish-republic">Second Polish Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
